    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d221">

                <pb facs="0313" n="301" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg301"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/2182: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador to the Yugoslav Government in Exile</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-24T18:00:00+02:00">February 24,
                            1944—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 25—8:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Yugoslav Series 44. In a talk which I had with the Soviet Ambassador<anchor
                        n="11" xml:id="d221-f1"/> this morning he told me confidentially that an
                    emissary or emissaries from Bulgaria might be arriving here shortly for
                    conversation in which he and I and our British colleague<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d221-f2"/> might be <pb facs="0314" n="302" xml:id="pg302"/>
                    expected to join. This is independent of a similar Rumanian project<anchor
                        n="13" xml:id="d221-f3"/> which has been the subject of messages between
                    Department and this Embassy (the last of these being the Department’s No. 21
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">1</hi>] to the Legation Cairo of January 5, 9
                        p.m.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d221-f4"/>). I have not heard of it from any
                    other source and if the Department has information which can be given me I would
                    appreciate instructions.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d221-f1" n="11" xml:id="d221-f1-content">
                <p>Nikolay Vasilyevich Novikov.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f2" n="12" xml:id="d221-f2-content">
                <p>R. C. Skrine Stevenson, British Ambassador to the Yugoslav Government in Exile in
                    Egypt.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f3" n="13" xml:id="d221-f3-content">
                <p>For Rumanian overtures, see telegram 84, January 11, 10 a.m., from Moscow, <ref
                        target="frus1944v04">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>
                    </ref>, section under Rumania entitled “Negotiations leading to signing of
                    armistice …”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f4" n="14" xml:id="d221-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
